#
# Consumed as `verible-verilog-lint --waiver_files=...` by tests/run-style.sh.
#
# This file is for findings that are correct about the code but wrong about what the
# code should be. A rule that is wrong *everywhere* belongs in .rules.verible_lint
# instead, disabled once with its reason; this file is for the narrow exceptions.
#
# Two rules for editing it, the same two verilator-waivers.vlt sets:
#
#   1. Every waiver carries its reason. A waiver with no reason is indistinguishable
#      from a bug someone hid.
#   2. Every waiver is scoped as narrowly as the syntax allows. --regex matches the
#      text of the offending line, so it can name the exact identifiers being excused;
#      a bare --location would waive the whole rule for the whole file and hide the
#      next occurrence in code nobody has looked at.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The DE0-Nano board's own port names
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# CLOCK_50, GPIO0, GPIO1 and LED are the only signals in the design that are not
# free to be renamed. DomesdayDuplicator.qsf binds them by name across 164 lines of
# pin-location and I/O-standard assignment, and the .SDC creates the 50 MHz clock
# with `[get_ports CLOCK_50]`. Renaming them means editing every one of those lines
# in step, and a single typo yields a board that programs successfully and drives the
# wrong pin — a failure that looks like broken hardware, not like a typo.
#
# They are also not this project's names to choose: they are Terasic's, printed in
# the DE0-Nano documentation that anyone wiring the board reads alongside this
# source.
#
# Scoped to the four names and to the top level, so a new ALL_CAPS signal anywhere —
# including a new port on this same module — is still reported.
waive --rule=signal-name-style --regex="\b(CLOCK_50|GPIO0|GPIO1|LED)\b" --location=".*DomesdayDuplicator\.v"
